		'Tokens, context windows, pre-training, fine-tuning, temperature and why models make things up: explained with diagrams in words.',
		'<h3>Tokens</h3><p>Models read text as tokens: small chunks of about 3–4 characters in English (“learning” might be “learn” + “ing”). Prices and limits are counted in tokens. A rough rule: 1,000 tokens ≈ 750 English words. Hindi and other scripts often use more tokens for the same meaning.</p><h3>The context window</h3><p>This is the model’s short-term memory for one conversation: your messages, its replies, any files. When it is full, the oldest parts drop out or get summarised. That is why very long chats “forget” early instructions. Restate key rules or start a new chat.</p><h3>Three stages of training</h3><ol><li><strong>Pre-training</strong>: predict the next token on a huge amount of public text and code. The model learns language, facts and reasoning patterns.</li><li><strong>Instruction tuning</strong>: learn to follow requests from examples of good answers.</li><li><strong>Preference tuning (RLHF and similar)</strong>: people rank answers, and the model learns to prefer helpful, honest and safe ones.</li></ol><h3>Temperature</h3><p>A setting for randomness. Low (0–0.3): focused and repeatable, good for code and facts. High (0.8+): more varied, good for ideas and stories. Chat apps choose it for you; APIs let you set it.</p><h3>Why hallucinations happen</h3><p>The model produces the <em>most plausible</em> text, not the <em>verified</em> text. If it never saw a fact, it may invent something that sounds right. To reduce this: give the source text in the prompt (“answer only from this document”), ask it to say “I don’t know”, or use a tool with web search and citations.</p><h3>Reasoning models</h3><p>Some models “think” before answering: they write hidden working steps. They are slower but much better at maths, code and multi-step problems. Use them for hard problems and fast models for simple tasks.</p><h3>Multimodal</h3><p>Modern models can read images, PDFs and audio, and some can create images or speech. The same prompting rules apply: be specific about the goal and the format.</p>'
