		'Role, task, context, examples, format and checks: one simple framework, with before-and-after examples.',
		'<h3>The framework</h3><ol><li><strong>Role</strong>: “You are a senior Java interviewer.”</li><li><strong>Task</strong>: one clear verb: write, explain, compare, fix, plan.</li><li><strong>Context</strong>: who it is for, your level, constraints, deadline.</li><li><strong>Examples</strong>: one sample of the style or output you like.</li><li><strong>Format</strong>: bullets, table, JSON, word count, language.</li><li><strong>Checks</strong>: “Ask me 3 questions first if anything is unclear” or “List assumptions.”</li></ol><h3>Before</h3><pre>Write a LinkedIn post about my project.</pre><h3>After</h3><pre>You are a tech career coach.\nWrite a LinkedIn post (120–150 words) about my final-year project:\na Spring Boot + React app that books college lab slots.\nAudience: recruiters for fresher Java roles in India.\nTone: confident, not boastful. Include 1 metric (it cut booking time from 2 days to 5 minutes).\nEnd with a question to invite comments. Add 4 relevant hashtags.</pre><h3>Power moves</h3><ul><li><strong>Iterate</strong>: “Make it shorter”, “More formal”, “Give 3 versions.”</li><li><strong>Self-review</strong>: “Now critique your answer like a strict reviewer and improve it.”</li><li><strong>Delimiters</strong>: put pasted text between <code>"""</code> so the model knows what is data and what is instruction.</li><li><strong>Ask for reasoning on hard tasks</strong>: “Think step by step, then give the final answer in one line.”</li></ul><h3>Common mistakes</h3><ul><li>Several tasks in one sentence. Split them.</li><li>No audience. “For a 10-year-old” and “for a CTO” give totally different answers.</li><li>Accepting the first answer. The second or third try is usually better.</li></ul>'
