		'What never to paste into a chatbot, how to fact-check AI, using AI honestly in college and at work.',
		'<h3>Never paste</h3><ul><li>Passwords, OTPs, API keys, bank or card details.</li><li>Aadhaar, PAN, passport numbers, medical reports of other people.</li><li>Your company’s private code or customer data, unless your company approves that tool.</li></ul><p>Many free chat tools may use conversations to improve their models. Check the settings for “improve the model” or “chat history” and turn it off for sensitive work.</p><h3>Fact-check in 60 seconds</h3><ol><li>Ask for sources, then open them yourself. Fake links and fake paper titles are common.</li><li>Check numbers, dates, prices and laws against an official site.</li><li>For code: run it, read it, and test edge cases. AI code can look right and be wrong.</li></ol><h3>College and exams</h3><p>Use AI as a tutor: ask for explanations, practice questions and feedback on <em>your</em> answer. Submitting AI-written work as your own breaks most college rules and teaches you nothing for interviews. A good rule: if you could not explain it without the AI, you have not learned it yet.</p><h3>At work</h3><ul><li>Follow your company’s AI policy and use approved tools.</li><li>You are responsible for what you send: review AI drafts before they go to clients.</li><li>Tell people when content is AI-generated if it matters to them (images of people, quotes, reviews).</li></ul><h3>Deepfakes and scams</h3><p>Voices and videos can be faked. If a “boss” or “relative” urgently asks for money or OTPs by call or video, hang up and call them back on a known number.</p>'
