		'rag-from-scratch',
		'RAG From Scratch: Build “Chat With Your PDFs” in Python',
		'Build with AI',
		'Intermediate',
		true,
		'Chunking, embeddings, a vector search and grounded answers with citations: a complete, working mini-project.',
		'<h3>What you build</h3><p>A command-line app that answers questions about your PDF notes and shows which page each answer came from. This is <strong>retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)</strong>: find the relevant text first, then let the model answer only from it.</p><h3>Step 1: extract and chunk</h3><pre>from pypdf import PdfReader\n\ndef chunks(path, size=800, overlap=150):\n    out = []\n    for page_no, page in enumerate(PdfReader(path).pages, start=1):\n        text = " ".join((page.extract_text() or "").split())\n        for i in range(0, len(text), size - overlap):\n            out.append({"page": page_no, "text": text[i:i + size]})\n    return out</pre><p>Overlap keeps sentences that cross a boundary findable. 500–1,000 characters per chunk is a good start.</p><h3>Step 2: embed</h3><p>An embedding turns text into a list of numbers so that similar meanings are close together. Use any embedding model (a hosted API or a local one such as a sentence-transformers model):</p><pre>from sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\nmodel = SentenceTransformer("all-MiniLM-L6-v2")\nvectors = model.encode([c["text"] for c in docs], normalize_embeddings=True)</pre><h3>Step 3: search</h3><pre>import numpy as np\n\ndef search(question, k=4):\n    q = model.encode([question], normalize_embeddings=True)[0]\n    scores = vectors @ q            # cosine similarity (vectors are normalised)\n    best = np.argsort(-scores)[:k]\n    return [docs[i] for i in best]</pre><p>For thousands of chunks this is enough. For millions, use a vector database (FAISS, pgvector, Qdrant, Chroma).</p><h3>Step 4: grounded prompt</h3><pre>context = "\\n\\n".join(f"[p.{c[\'page\']}] {c[\'text\']}" for c in hits)\nprompt = f"""Answer the question using ONLY the context.\nCite pages like [p.3]. If the answer is not in the context, say "Not in the notes."\n\nContext:\n{context}\n\nQuestion: {question}"""</pre><p>Send <code>prompt</code> to any chat model API and print the answer.</p><h3>Step 5: evaluate</h3><ul><li>Write 15 questions you know the answers to, including 3 that are <em>not</em> in the notes.</li><li>Score: correct answer, correct page cited, honest “Not in the notes”.</li><li>Tune chunk size, k and the prompt until you pass 13/15.</li></ul><h3>Upgrades</h3><ul><li><strong>Hybrid search</strong>: combine keyword (BM25) and vector scores for names and codes.</li><li><strong>Re-ranking</strong>: retrieve 20, re-rank to the best 4 with a cross-encoder.</li><li><strong>Metadata filters</strong>: subject, semester, date.</li><li><strong>A web UI</strong>: Streamlit or a Spring Boot API with a React page.</li></ul><p>Put this project on GitHub with a short README and a demo GIF. It is one of the most asked-about projects in AI engineer interviews.</p>'
