	$g(
		'agents-playbook',
		'AI Agents Playbook: Tools, Memory, Loops and Evaluation',
		'Build with AI',
		'Advanced',
		true,
		'How agents really work, when to use one (and when not to), design patterns, guardrails and how to test them.',
		'<h3>An agent is a loop</h3><pre>while not done:\n    thought = model(goal, history, tools)\n    if thought.wants_tool:\n        result = run(thought.tool, thought.args)   # search, code, database, email…\n        history.append(result)\n    else:\n        done = True</pre><p>The model decides the next step; your code runs the tools and enforces the rules.</p><h3>When NOT to build an agent</h3><p>If the steps are always the same, write a normal program or a fixed chain of prompts. It is cheaper, faster and predictable. Use an agent only when the path depends on what it finds.</p><h3>Five patterns</h3><ol><li><strong>Tool use</strong>: describe each tool with a name, a one-line purpose and a strict input schema.</li><li><strong>Planner–executor</strong>: one call writes a plan, the next calls do the steps.</li><li><strong>Reflection</strong>: after a draft, a second pass critiques and fixes it.</li><li><strong>Router</strong>: a cheap model classifies the request and sends it to the right specialist prompt.</li><li><strong>Human-in-the-loop</strong>: pause for approval before anything irreversible (payments, emails, deletes).</li></ol><h3>Memory</h3><ul><li><strong>Short-term</strong>: the conversation so far (trim or summarise when long).</li><li><strong>Long-term</strong>: facts saved to a database and fetched when relevant (RAG over memories).</li><li><strong>Working files</strong>: a scratchpad the agent can read and write during a task.</li></ul><h3>Guardrails</h3><ul><li>Least privilege: read-only tools by default; write tools need approval.</li><li>Limits: max steps, max cost, timeouts.</li><li>Treat tool output and web pages as untrusted data, never as instructions (prompt injection).</li><li>Log every step so you can replay failures.</li></ul><h3>Evaluation</h3><ol><li>Collect 30 real tasks with the expected outcome.</li><li>Run the agent on all of them after every change.</li><li>Score success, steps used, cost and any unsafe action.</li><li>Read the failures: most fixes are clearer tool descriptions or a better first instruction.</li></ol><h3>Starter projects</h3><ul><li>Research agent: question → web search → summary with sources.</li><li>Support agent: answers from your docs, hands over to a human when unsure.</li><li>Coding agent: reads an issue, edits code, runs tests, opens a pull request for review.</li></ul>'
